ts:**\n  - The handle is thick and ergonomically shaped, likely to provide a secure grip even when wet or gloved.\n  - The blade’s curvature and thickness suggest it is designed for both chopping and slicing, making it versatile for outdoor or survival tasks.\n\n- **Context of Use:**\n  - This knife is handled by both men, with one man (in the black jacket) demonstrating its features and the other (in the camouflage jacket) examining it closely.\n  - The man in the camouflage jacket appears to be explaining or showcasing the knife, possibly highlighting its craftsmanship or functionality.\n  - In later frames, the man in the camouflage jacket is seen holding the knife outdoors, suggesting it may be used for practical tasks in the field.\n\n---\n\n### **Knife 2: The Smaller, Conventional Knife**\n\n- **Shape & Size:**\n  - This knife is much smaller and more compact, with a straight, sharp blade.\n  - The blade is thinner and more pointed, indicating it is designed for precision tasks such as cutting, carving, or skinning.\n\n- **Materials:**\n  - **Blade:** The blade is also made of steel, with a polished, reflective finish.\n  - **Handle:** The handle is dark, possibly made of synthetic material or treated wood, and has a more modern, streamlined design.\n\n- **Unique Design Elements:**\n  - The handle is contoured for a comfortable grip, with a slight taper towards the end.\n  - The blade’s sharp point and straight edge suggest it is optimized for detailed work rather than heavy-duty tasks.\n\n- **Context of Use:**\n  - This knife is primarily handled by the man in the black jacket, who appears to be comparing it to the larger knife.\n  - The man in the camouflage jacket examines the smaller knife closely, possibly evaluating its suitability for specific tasks.\n\n---\n\n### **Comparison and Discussion**\n\n- **Differences in Functionality:**\n  - The large cleaver-style knife is clearly intended for heavy-duty tasks, such as chopping wood or processing game, whil