. The way it's attached to the blade (visible in frames 2, 3, 4, 6, 7) appears to be a traditional method, possibly with a tang and rivets or a simple wedge, rather than a modern molded plastic or composite handle.\n\n3.  **Context and Comparison:** In frames 2, 3, 4, 6, and 7, the man in the black jacket is holding a different knife. This second knife has a more conventional, straight-edged, pointed blade and a dark, likely synthetic or metal handle. He is actively comparing the two knives, holding them side-by-side. This act of comparison, combined with the unique design of the first knife, strongly suggests that the Ulu-style knife is being presented as the 'local' or 'traditional' one, while the other is a more modern or standard tool.\n\n4.  **Presentation and Emphasis:** In frames 8 and 9, the man in the black jacket (who was previously comparing the knives) is now holding the Ulu-style knife alone and appears to be speaking about it, possibly explaining its features or cultural significance. He holds it up for the camera, emphasizing its importance. This presentation style further supports the idea that this is the 'local knife' being showcased.\n\n5.  **Lack of Modern Branding:** Neither knife has any visible branding or text that would indicate a specific manufacturer. The absence of such markings on the Ulu-style knife, combined with its traditional design, reinforces the idea that it is a locally made or traditional tool rather than a mass-produced item.\n\nIn conclusion, the knife with the wide, curved blade and wooden handle is almost certainly the 'local knife' being referred to. Its design is characteristic of the traditional Alaskan Ulu, and the context of the video frames—showing it being compared to a more conventional knife and then presented alone for explanation—confirms its status as the culturally significant, local tool."}], 'formatted_responses': ["\n=== Batch 0-9 (9 frames) ===\nFrames: /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/KbahC-QCKU8/fra